      "exp_en": "This is the most complete and accurate answer. Moxibustion is not generally contraindicated with osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, or congestive heart failure. However, the age of the patient, constitution, and overall debility can help determine whether it should be applied. It is contraindicated in patients with paralysis, hemiplegia, and diabetes or any disorder which can be associated with decreased sensitivity to pain. The CNT manual recommends considering other options for patients with a history of significant asthma or patients who have a reaction to smoke. Inhalation of any kind of smoke can cause harm, especially in lung disorders and with weak patients. It is important to have good ventilation and air filtering. Leg numbness with decreased sensation is typically due to nerve and muscle damage. which can be linked to nerve compression, Buerger's disease, Leriche's syndrome, and peripheral neuropathy. Peripheral Artery Disease (PAD) is a narrowing of the arteries in the legs. PAD has pain numbness and weakness that occur when walking or exercising and go away with rest. Nerve compression can be caused by joint swelling, cysts, or trauma. Patients can often experience leg numbness, pain, tingling, and prickling in the legs. Peripheral neuropathies can range from carpal tunnel syndrome to nerve damage linked to diabetes. Patients with chronic peripheral neuropathies often lose their ability to sense temperature and pain. Neuropathy is a general term used to describe a variety of nerve problems and damage. Nerve compression more specifically points to conditions where excessive pressure is applied to an isolated nerve. In Buerger's disease, blood vessels in the legs become inflamed leading to severe numbness causing the patient to experience tingling and burning in the legs, poor circulation, and discoloration or numbness of the feet. Another syndrome is Leriche's syndrome which is a disorder caused by blockages in the aorta of the heart. This blockage severely restricts blood flow to the legs resulting in numbness and decreased pain sensitivity. Patients with this syndrome commonly experience severe fatigue and coldness in the lower extremities. CNT Manual, 7th Edition, page 103",
