      "exp_en": "Blood stasis or Blood depletion can be involved with amenorrhea. This patient has the Deficient Blood type. A weak Spleen will lead to Blood depletion which then fails to nourish the ren and chong mai. Dysfunction of the transformation and transportation function of the Spleen produces sallow complexion, loose stools, fatigue, and poor appetite. As the Blood or yin is depleted there will be symptoms of dryness (skin, mouth). The points BL-20 and ST-36 strengthen the Earth to produce qi and Blood. SP-6 strengthens the Spleen, regulates the Liver, and nourishes the Kidneys and is a topmost point in treating any GYN disorder. As the Liver stores Blood and Liver Blood nourishes the uterus, BL-18 is selected to promote the Blood in the Liver. This prescription did not accomplish its goal because an important aspect of chronic amenorrhea is the involvement of the Kidneys. Blood regulation results from the proper functioning of the Spleen, Liver, and Kidneys. In this patient the chronic nature of the problem, the weak knees, and dizziness (also cause by xu Blood) point to the Kidneys which were not sufficiently addressed in the prescription. BL-23 tonifies the Kidney yin and yang and benefits the essence. REN-4 strengthens original qi, benefits essence and uterus, nourishes the Kidneys, and fortifies the Spleen. The combination of BL-32 and SP-8 is more appropriate for treating dysmenorrhea due to Blood stasis. The other point combos aim more at strengthening the Spleen and Stomach qi. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion, 3rd Edition, 2010, page 477; CAM, 4th Edition, page 577",
