        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "Does not allow the affected hip to move",
          "hant": "患側髖關節完全無法活動",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "This is called the Thomas test which is a physical examination test to rule out hip flexion contracture and psoas syndrome. The patient is supine and holds the uninvolved knee close to the chest, while allowing the involved leg to lie flat. Holding the knee to the chest flattens out the lumbar lordosis, stabilizing the pelvis. If the iliopsoas muscle is contracted, the lower extremity on the involved side will be unable to fully extend at the hip. This is a positive Thomas test. Flexion deformity can be masked by an increase, rather than a flattening, of the lumbar lordosis and anterior pelvic tilt. Bate's Guide to Physical Examination, 13th Edition, page 799-800; Bate's Guide to Physical Examination, 10th Edition, page 622-623",
      "exp_hant": "此稱為托馬斯測試（Thomas test），是用以排除髖關節屈曲攣縮及腰肌症候群的理學檢查。病患採仰臥姿，將未患側膝關節抱近胸部，同時使患側下肢平放於床面。將膝關節抱近胸部可使腰椎前凸變平坦，藉此穩定骨盆。若髂腰肌發生攣縮，患側下肢的髖關節將無法完全伸直，此即為陽性的托馬斯測試。屈曲攣縮可能因腰椎前凸增加（而非變平坦）及骨盆前傾而被掩蓋，未能被察覺。《Bate's Guide to Physical Examination》，第13版，799-800頁；《Bate's Guide to Physical Examination》，第10版，622-623頁"
