      "exp_en": "The red flag is increased confusion in an elderly person occurring days, weeks, or months after a minor head trauma. Chronic subdural hematoma is assumed present until proven otherwise. The orientation of the patient to time, person, and place may be normal or affected in this condition. There can be slurred speech, balance problems, difficulty walking, headache, or lethargy. In more advanced cases there can be stupor and severe confusion. In this case the neurological presentation is not severe and can be referred to a primary care physician who will then refer him to a neurosurgeon. If the confusion or other neurological signs and symptoms are rapidly progressing but with stable cardio-pulmonary systems, the patient should be transported directly to an ED by the son. If there are also cardiac and lung symptoms (low BP, SOB, or chest pain) then 911 should be called. An Acupuncturist's Guide to Medical Red Flags and Referrals, 2012, Anzaldua, pages 13-14",
      "exp_hant": "此案例的警訊為老年人在輕微頭部外傷後數天、數週或數月間出現意識混亂加重的情形。在排除其他可能性之前，應假設患者為慢性硬腦膜下血腫。此病症患者對時間、人物、地點的定向能力可能正常，也可能受影響，並可能出現口齒不清、平衡失調、行走困難、頭痛或嗜睡等症狀，病情較嚴重時甚至可能出現嗜睡昏沉與嚴重意識混亂。本案例的神經學表現尚不嚴重，可轉診至基層醫師，再由其轉介至神經外科醫師。若意識混亂或其他神經學徵狀迅速惡化，但心肺功能仍穩定，則應由兒子直接將患者送往急診室；若同時出現心肺症狀（如低血壓、呼吸困難或胸痛），則應撥打911。《針灸師醫療警訊與轉診指南》，2012年，Anzaldua著，第13-14頁"
    },
