        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "coconut water or milk",
          "hant": "椰子水或牛奶",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "This is the most complete and accurate answer. In heat cramps there are painful muscles spasms most often in the legs and abdomen due to fluid and electrolyte loss. Heat cramps are the first sign that the body is reacting to excess heat. If not treated, heat cramps can develop into heat exhaustion and then heat stroke. The strategy is move the person to a cool place to rest and have the person sip on a sports drink with electrolytes, drink coconut water or drink milk. The person can also slightly stretch the muscle. When the cramping stops the person can resume normal activity as long as there no other signs. A person with heat cramps should not be given salt tablets to make up for a loss of sodium. Salt tablets will not work with heat cramps as salt promotes fluid loss from the body which will worsen the condition. First Aid/CPR/AED, Participant Manual, Red Cross, 2016, page 120",
      "exp_hant": "這是最完整且最準確的答案。熱痙攣是指因體液與電解質流失，而在腿部與腹部出現疼痛性肌肉痙攣，是身體對過度炎熱反應的最初徵兆。若未妥善處理，熱痙攣可能進一步惡化為熱衰竭，甚至熱中暑。處置原則是將患者移至陰涼處休息，並讓其小口啜飲含電解質的運動飲料，或飲用椰子水或牛奶，也可稍微伸展抽筋的肌肉。若痙攣停止且無其他症狀，患者即可恢復正常活動。熱痙攣患者不應服用鹽片來補充流失的鈉，因為鹽分反而會促使體內水分流失，使病情惡化，因此鹽片對熱痙攣並無幫助。《紅十字會急救／CPR／AED學員手冊》，2016年，第120頁"
    },
    {
      "number": 266,
      "question_en": "Which OTC antacid drugs are classifed as proton pump inhibitors?",
      "question_hant": "哪些非處方（OTC）制酸劑藥物屬於質子幫浦抑制劑？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "calcium carbonate, magnesium carbonate",
          "hant": "碳酸鈣、碳酸鎂",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "en": "omeprazole, esomeprazole",
          "hant": "奧美拉唑（omeprazole）、艾索美拉唑（esomeprazole）",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "magnesium hydroxide, aluminum hydroxide",
          "hant": "氫氧化鎂、氫氧化鋁",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "nizatidine, ranitidine",
          "hant": "尼扎替丁（nizatidine）、雷尼替丁（ranitidine）",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "Prilosec (omeprazole), Prevacid (lansoprazole), and Nexium (esomeprazole) are classified as proton pump inhibitors (PPIs). They all end with -azole. Drugs that end with idine are H2 antagonist receptor drugs. Magnesium hydroxide, aluminum hydroxide, calcium carbonate, and magnesium carbonate are acid neutralizers. Cells in the stomach make gastric acid with enzymes that are called proton pumps. PPIs reduce the production of acid by blocking this enzyme in the stomach wall that produces acid. PPIs block acid secretion by binding to these pumps. This decreases the amount of stomach acid can help ulcers heal and reflux to subside. Ongoing research is investigating the association between PPIs, reduced calcium absorption, and an increased risk of bone fractures. H2 blockers block histamine 2 on the stomach wall. Histamine triggers acid production. H2 blockers may be effective for up to 12 hours. The effect of PPIs may last from 24 hours to three days. H2 blockers often work within one hour. PPIs work better when taken 30 minutes before your meal or on an empty stomach. When taken right before or after eating, the stomach has already released most of the acid that the drug works on preventing. tcmtests.com, February 2019",
      "exp_hant": "Prilosec（奧美拉唑）、Prevacid（蘭索拉唑）及Nexium（艾索美拉唑）皆屬於質子幫浦抑制劑（PPIs），藥名字尾皆為「-azole」。字尾為「-idine」的藥物則屬於H2受體拮抗劑。氫氧化鎂、氫氧化鋁、碳酸鈣及碳酸鎂則屬於中和胃酸的制酸劑。胃部細胞是藉由稱為質子幫浦的酵素製造胃酸，PPIs透過阻斷胃壁上此酵素來減少胃酸分泌，其作用機轉是與這些幫浦結合以阻斷胃酸分泌，進而降低胃酸量，有助於潰瘍癒合及緩解逆流症狀。目前仍有研究持續探討PPIs與鈣質吸收降低及骨折風險增加之間的關聯。H2阻斷劑則是阻斷胃壁上的組織胺第二型受體，而組織胺會促進胃酸分泌。H2阻斷劑的藥效可持續長達12小時，PPIs的藥效則可持續24小時至三天。H2阻斷劑通常於一小時內見效，PPIs則建議於飯前30分鐘或空腹時服用效果較佳；若在進食前後才服用，胃部往往已釋出大部分該藥物原本要抑制的胃酸。tcmtests.com，2019年2月"
    },
    {
      "number": 267,
      "question_en": "OSHA regulations require hand washing with soap and running water after the removal of Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) such as gloves, face shields, or eye protection. When should this washing of hands be done?",
      "question_hant": "OSHA法規要求在脫除個人防護裝備（PPE，如手套、面罩或護目鏡）後，須以肥皂及流動清水洗手。此洗手動作應於何時進行？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "Before seeing next patient",
          "hant": "於看下一位患者之前",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "en": "Before the handling of food",
          "hant": "於處理食物之前",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "At the end of the day",
          "hant": "於一天結束時",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "Immediately after removal",
          "hant": "脫除後立即",
          "correct": true
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "Hand washing should be done: 1) upon completion of required tasks and before leaving the office. 2) Immediately after removal of gloves or other personal protective equipment. 3) Upon contact or when there is visible contamination with blood or other potentially infectious material. 4) Before and after patient contact. 5) Before eating, drinking, smoking, applying cosmetics, or handling contact lenses. 6) Before and after using the bathroom. 7) Before any activities in which hand contact is made with mucous membranes, the eyes, or breaks in the skin (eg, cuts, abrasions, wounds). Acupuncture Risk Management, CMS Press, Kailin, 1997 page 62",
      "exp_hant": "洗手應於下列時機進行：1) 完成必要工作、離開診間之前；2) 脫除手套或其他個人防護裝備後立即進行；3) 接觸到血液或其他可能具感染性物質，或發現明顯污染時；4) 接觸患者前後；5) 進食、飲水、抽菸、化妝或配戴隱形眼鏡之前；6) 如廁前後；7) 從事任何會使手部接觸黏膜、眼睛，或皮膚破損處（如切割傷、擦傷、傷口）的活動之前。《針灸風險管理》，CMS Press，Kailin著，1997年，第62頁"
    },
    {
      "number": 268,
      "question_en": "What is an accurate comparison between a motor neuron and a motor nerve?",
      "question_hant": "關於運動神經元與運動神經之間的比較，何者正確？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "both neurons and nerves are located in the peripheral and central nervous system",
          "hant": "神經元與神經皆存在於周邊及中樞神經系統中",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "en": "a neuron conducts electrochemical impulses and the nerve receives them",
          "hant": "神經元傳導電化學衝動，而神經負責接收這些衝動",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "both are composed of a single axon, cell body, and dendrites",
          "hant": "兩者皆由單一軸突、細胞體及樹突組成",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "a neuron is a single nerve cell and a nerve is a group of neurons",
          "hant": "神經元是單一神經細胞，而神經則是一群神經元的組合",
          "correct": true
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "Both nerves and neuron are part of the nervous system. Nerves are found only in the peripheral nervous system (sensory receptors, sensory neurons, motor neurons). Neurons are found in both peripheral and central nervous systems (brain and spinal cord). Both nerves and neuron transmit nerve impulses. The neuron is the cell that makes up the nervous system. The nerve is a bundle of neurons. A neuron is composed of an axon, cell body, and dendrites. A nerve is a bundle of axons of a large number of neurons. A motor neuron is a single nerve cell (body, axon, dendrites) and a motor nerve is a group of motor neurons that innervate a muscle. The neuron generates electrochemical nerve impulses whereas nerves provide the environment for conducting the electrochemical nerve impulses. A nerve is a bundle of neurons along with their connective tissue sheaths, blood vessels, and lymphatics whereas a neuron is a cell of the nervous system that conducts nerve impulses. There are three types of neurons: sensory neurons, motor neurons, and interneurons (they connect motor with sensory neurons). There are two types of nerves: cranial nerves, spinal nerves. The key difference between cranial and spinal nerves is that the cranial nerves (12 pairs) come from the brain while the spinal nerves (31 pairs) come from the spinal cord. Except for the the vagus nerve (CNX), cranial nerves coordinate activities associated with the head and neck (eyes, mouth, face and other parts of the head). The spinal nerves coordinate the activities associated with all the body parts below the neck (muscles, organs, glands). Some of the cranial nerves are sensory, some are motor nerves and very few are mixed nerves. All of the spinal nerves are mixed nerves because they have both sensory and motor fibers. tcmtests.com, August 2020",
      "exp_hant": "神經與神經元皆屬於神經系統的一部分。神經僅存在於周邊神經系統（包括感覺受器、感覺神經元、運動神經元），而神經元則同時存在於周邊神經系統與中樞神經系統（腦部及脊髓）。神經與神經元皆負責傳導神經衝動：神經元是構成神經系統的細胞，神經則是神經元的集合體。神經元由軸突、細胞體及樹突組成，神經則是大量神經元軸突所形成的束狀結構。運動神經元是單一神經細胞（含細胞體、軸突、樹突），運動神經則是一群支配某一肌肉的運動神經元。神經元負責產生電化學神經衝動，神經則提供傳導這些電化學神經衝動的環境；神經是由神經元連同其結締組織鞘、血管及淋巴管所組成的束狀構造，神經元則是神經系統中負責傳導神經衝動的細胞。神經元可分為三種類型：感覺神經元、運動神經元，以及連接運動與感覺神經元的中間神經元。神經則分為兩種類型：腦神經與脊神經。腦神經與脊神經的主要差異在於：腦神經（共12對）發自腦部，脊神經（共31對）則發自脊髓。除迷走神經（第十對腦神經）外，腦神經主要負責協調頭頸部（眼、口、臉及頭部其他部位）相關的活動，脊神經則負責協調頸部以下所有身體部位（肌肉、器官、腺體）相關的活動。部分腦神經屬於感覺神經，部分屬於運動神經，僅少數為混合神經；而所有脊神經皆為混合神經，因其同時具有感覺與運動纖維。tcmtests.com，2020年8月"
    },
