      "exp_en": "The drugs phenelzine (Nardil), isocarboxazid (Marplan), selegiline (Emsam), and tranylcypromine (Parnate) belong to the class of drugs called Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs) which can treat severe depression and Parkinson's disease. MAOIs can interact with foods and drinks that contain high levels of tyramine, a naturally occurring substances in certain foods. These foods include aged cheeses, fermented foods (sauerkraut, kimchi, pickles), cured meats, red wine, draft beer, and fermented soy products (soy sauce, miso). This interaction of tyramine with MAOIs can cause dangerously high blood pressure. mayoclinic.org",
