      "exp_en": "This is the most complete and accurate answer. The Lachman test is for assessing the knee for an anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) tear. In the Apley Scratch Test the patient is asked to touch the opposite scapula from above and from below. In the Neer's Impingement Sign, the examiner, with one hand, presses on the scapula to prevent scapular motion and raises the patient's arm with the other. In the Hawkins Impingement sign, the examiner flexes the patient's shoulder and elbow at 90 degrees with the palm facing down. Then examiner places one hand in the forearm and one the arm to rotate the arm internally. Bate's Guide to Physical Examination, 13th Edition, page 769-70; Bate's Guide to Physical Examination, 10th Edition, page 597",
