      "exp_en": "In this particular situation, you cannot make additions to the medical notes to help the patient get a favorable ruling. The acupuncturist needs to be aware of their bias and whether they are being impartial. If the lower back pain was not a chief complaint, the acupuncturist cannot say, for example, that the patient had low back pain based on using BL-23 in their treatment, even if a misinformed lawyer advises you. As a health care practitioner you have an ethical duty to have a professional mindset and to give an accurate assessment. You do not present yourself as an advocate for the patient's desired outcome for a favorable settlement. The practitioner needs discuss with the patient about their future doctor-patient relationship if there is an unfavorable outcome. In this situation the guidelines to follow include: 1) Medical records are not altered. However, it is acceptable to add information that corrects an earlier entry. The amended entry should be signed/initialed and dated; 2) The patient is the only person that has the right to determine who can have access to their protected health information (PHI) and services rendered. If the patient is a minor then their legal guardian can give consent to share the PHI; 3) Protocols need to be put in place before starting a practice that address issues of informed consent and confidentiality. One variable is whether or not you are legally required to participate in the hearing. What are your local and state laws that determine how PHI is handled in Worker Compensation cases. If required to attend, do you participate in the hearing on your own time or do you bill the Worker Compensation agency for your participation? There are other issues to consider if you are not legally required to attend. If you conclude that what you disclose in the hearing will not help the patient get the settlement that they want, you need to think through a different set of possibilities. Let the patient know that it is up to them to determine whether you participate in the hearing. More importantly, if the patient wants you to give your assessment, they need to know that your involvement does not guarantee the outcome that they want. Ethics Handbook for Energy Healing Practitioners, Kindle Edition, Feinstein, page 88",
      "exp_hant": "在此情況下，您不能透過修改病歷紀錄，來協助患者獲得有利的裁決結果。針灸師必須意識到自身可能存在的偏見，並確保保持中立客觀。若下背痛並非最初的主訴，即便律師（可能缺乏相關認知）建議您這麼做，針灸師也不能僅因治療中使用了腎俞穴，就聲稱該患者曾有下背痛。作為醫療從業人員，您有倫理上的責任，須保持專業立場並提供準確的評估，而不應以患者所期望達成的和解結果作為代言立場。施術者應與患者討論，若結果不如預期，將如何影響未來的醫病關係。在此情況下，應遵循以下原則：1）病歷不得竄改，但可加入更正先前紀錄的資訊，此更正項目應簽名（或蓋章）並註明日期；2）唯有患者本人有權決定誰可以取得其個人健康資訊（PHI）及所接受服務之相關內容，若患者為未成年人，則由其法定監護人代為同意釋出PHI；3）在開業之前應先建立相關規範，處理告知後同意與保密性等議題；其中一項考量因素是，您是否依法必須出席該聽證會。應了解當地及所屬州對工傷賠償案件中PHI處理方式的相關法規；若依法須出席，您應以個人時間參與，或是向工傷賠償機構收取出席費用？若您並非依法必須出席，仍有其他需考量的因素：若您認為在聽證會上所揭露的內容並無助於患者取得所期望的和解結果，則需重新考慮不同的因應方式。應讓患者了解，是否讓您出席聽證會取決於他們自己的決定；更重要的是，若患者希望您提供評估意見，應讓其了解您的參與並不保證能達成他們所期望的結果。《能量療癒從業人員倫理手冊》，Kindle版，Feinstein著，第88頁"
