    {
      "number": 356,
      "question_en": "The American Cancer Society recommends screening individuals of average risk for colon cancer at what age?",
      "question_hant": "美國癌症協會建議一般風險族群應於幾歲開始接受大腸癌篩檢？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "55 years of age",
          "hant": "55歲",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "en": "30 years of age",
          "hant": "30歲",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "50 years of age",
          "hant": "50歲",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "45 years of age",
          "hant": "45歲",
          "correct": true
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "The American Cancer Society guideline says colorectal cancer screening should begin at age 45 for people at average risk. Adults ages 45 and older with an average risk of colorectal cancer can undergo regular screening with either a stool-based test or a structural (visual) exam, depending on patient preference and test availability. All positive results on a non-colonoscopy screening test should be followed up with timely colonoscopy. Average-risk adults in good health with a life expectancy of more than 10 years should continue colorectal cancer screening through age 75. Clinicians should individualize colorectal cancer screening decisions for individuals ages 76 through 85, based on patient preferences, life expectancy, and health status. Clinicians should discourage individuals over age 85 from continuing colorectal cancer screening. The recommended options for colorectal cancer screening are: 1) fecal immunochemical test (FIT) annually. 2) high sensitivity guaiac-based fecal occult blood test annually. 3) multi-target stool DNA test (mt-sDNA) every 3 years. 4) colonoscopy every 10 years. 5) CT colonography (CTC) every 5 years. And 6) flexible sigmoidoscopy (FS) every 5 years. The new guideline do not give a preference among the different screening test options. cancer.org",
      "exp_hant": "美國癌症協會指引指出，一般風險族群應自45歲開始接受大腸直腸癌篩檢。45歲以上、屬一般風險的成人可依患者偏好及檢測可行性，選擇糞便檢測或結構性（視覺）檢查進行定期篩檢。非大腸鏡篩檢結果為陽性者，均應及時接受大腸鏡檢查追蹤。健康狀況良好且預期壽命超過10年的一般風險成人，應持續接受大腸直腸癌篩檢至75歲。臨床醫師應根據患者偏好、預期壽命與健康狀況，為76至85歲的個案個別化決定篩檢與否。臨床醫師應勸阻85歲以上的個案繼續接受大腸直腸癌篩檢。建議的大腸直腸癌篩檢選項包括：1）每年進行糞便免疫化學檢測（FIT）。2）每年進行高敏感度愈創木脂糞便潛血檢測。3）每3年進行多標靶糞便DNA檢測（mt-sDNA）。4）每10年進行大腸鏡檢查。5）每5年進行電腦斷層大腸攝影（CTC）。以及6）每5年進行可彎曲乙狀結腸鏡檢查（FS）。新指引並未偏好任何一種篩檢方式。cancer.org"
    },
    {
      "number": 357,
      "question_en": "You are conferring with another acupuncturist regarding a depressed patient that both of you are treating. Your colleague says that he does not want to ask the patient about suicidal ideation because \"It might put ideas in her head about suicide.\" What is your best response?",
      "question_hant": "您正與另一位針灸師討論你們共同治療的一位憂鬱症患者。您的同事表示他不想詢問患者是否有自殺意念，因為「這可能會讓她產生自殺的念頭」。您最佳的回應為何？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "Actually, it's a myth that asking about suicide puts ideas into someone's head.",
          "hant": "其實，詢問自殺會讓人產生自殺念頭是一種迷思。",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "en": "If I were you, I'd ask the clinic director to talk to the patient about that subject.",
          "hant": "如果我是你，我會請診所主任去和患者談這個話題。",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "I'm glad you are thinking that way. She may not have thought of suicide before.",
          "hant": "我很高興你這樣想。她可能之前從未想過自殺。",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "You are correct but because of professional liability, we have to ask that question.",
          "hant": "你說得對，但基於專業責任風險，我們必須詢問這個問題。",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "Asking about suicidal thoughts does not \"give a person ideas\" and is, in fact, a professional responsibility similar to asking about chest pain in cardiac disorders. Talking openly leads to a decrease in isolation and can increase problem-solving alternatives for living. Patients have usually been already thinking about suicide. It is a myth that bringing up the topic will somehow cause someone to become suicidal. Liability is not the reason to ask patients about suicidal thoughts or plans for suicide. Asking about it is for the safety of the patient. Asking the clinic director to speak to the patient is an abdication of professional and ethical responsibility for keeping the patient safe. Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing, Townsend, 2017, Chapter 17",
      "exp_hant": "詢問自殺意念並不會『讓人產生念頭』，事實上這是一種專業責任，就如同詢問心臟疾病患者是否有胸痛一樣。公開的談話能減少孤立感，並增加解決生活困境的替代方案。患者通常早已在思考自殺。認為提起這個話題會導致某人變得有自殺傾向，是一種迷思。詢問患者自殺想法或計畫的原因並非法律責任風險，而是為了患者的安全。要求診所主任去與患者談話，是放棄了維護患者安全的專業與倫理責任。《精神衛生護理學》，Townsend著，2017年，第17章"
    },
