    {
      "number": 365,
      "question_en": "Which major sign that is within normal range of functioning indicates that the effects of shock on the body are controlled?",
      "question_hant": "哪一項維持在正常功能範圍內的主要徵象，顯示休克對身體的影響已受到控制？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "urinary output",
          "hant": "尿量",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "en": "respiratory rate",
          "hant": "呼吸速率",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "blood pressure",
          "hant": "血壓",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "heart rate",
          "hant": "心跳速率",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "In medicine, the word shock refers to a physical imbalance between the oxygen and nutrients the body needs and the oxygen and nutrients actually supplied to the cells. This can cause cellular dysfunction, organ failure, and can eventually lead to death. Shocks have three main stages: COMPENSATORY STAGE: When there is low and inadequate blood flow (perfusion) to nourish the cells and tissues, the body activates compensatory mechanisms to restore perfusion. These mechanisms can include physiological, neural, hormonal, and biochemical reactions. For example, hyperventilation causes an increased rate of breathing which can help increase cellular oxygen levels. Another mechanism is the catecholamine response in which hormones (epinephrine, norepinephrine) produced by the adrenals increase heart rate and increase blood pressure. Another mechanism is the release of the hormone vasopressin into the blood to help retain body fluids and trigger vasoconstriction. Emergency treatment here can prevent progressing to other stages. As long as the body manages to keep the blood pressure up, it is considered compensated shock. When the blood pressure drops, even in cases when that happens early, it then become decompensated shock and enters the progressive stage. decompensated or progressive shock. If decompensated shock is left untreated, it has a high likelihood of becoming fatal. PROGRESSIVE STAGE: When the mechanisms employed in stage one begin to fail, the body fails to maintain blood flow that nourishes tissues (perfusion). This results in acidosis and oxygen deprivation in the brain causing confusion, disorientation, and loss of consciousness. REFRACTORY STAGE: In this stage the effects of shock are irreversible. Brain damage and cell death are occurring, and death is imminent. Heart functioning continues to deteriorate, kidneys shut down, cells, tissues and organs begin dying. Blood pressure continues dropping until there is death. The Complementary Therapist's Guide to Red Flag Referrals, Stephenson, 2013, page 48",
      "exp_hant": "在醫學上，「休克」一詞指的是身體所需氧氣與營養與實際供應到細胞的氧氣與營養之間的生理失衡。這可導致細胞功能障礙、器官衰竭，最終可能導致死亡。休克有三個主要階段：代償期：當血流灌注不足以滋養細胞與組織時，身體會啟動代償機制以恢復灌注。這些機制可能包括生理性、神經性、荷爾蒙性與生化反應。例如，過度換氣會增加呼吸速率，有助於提升細胞氧氣濃度。另一種機制是兒茶酚胺反應，即腎上腺分泌的荷爾蒙（腎上腺素、正腎上腺素）會增加心跳速率並提高血壓。另一種機制是釋放抗利尿激素（vasopressin）進入血液，有助於保留體液並引發血管收縮。此階段的緊急治療可預防病情進展至其他階段。只要身體能維持血壓，即視為代償性休克。當血壓下降時，即便發生得較早，也會轉為失代償性休克，進入進展期，即失代償性或進展性休克。若失代償性休克未經治療，極有可能致命。進展期：當第一階段所採用的機制開始失效時，身體無法維持滋養組織所需的血流灌注，導致酸中毒與腦部缺氧，出現意識混亂、定向力障礙與意識喪失。頑固期：在此階段，休克的影響已不可逆。腦損傷與細胞死亡正在發生，死亡即將來臨。心臟功能持續惡化，腎臟衰竭，細胞、組織與器官開始死亡，血壓持續下降直到死亡。《補充療法師紅旗轉診指南》，Stephenson著，2013年，第48頁"
    },
    {
      "number": 366,
      "question_en": "What are the two types of anorexia nervosa?",
      "question_hant": "厭食症（anorexia nervosa）有哪兩種類型？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "binge eating type and obsessive-compulsive type",
          "hant": "暴食型與強迫型",
          "correct": false
