      "exp_en": "SPINAL STENOSIS is a narrowing of the intervertebral spaces in the spine. The narrowing puts pressure on the spinal nerves. Spinal stenosis occurs most frequently in the lower back and neck. Spinal stenosis is most commonly caused by wear-and-tear changes in the spine related degenerative disc disease (DDD) and osteoarthritis. X-rays and MRI can help confirm the diagnosis. Symptoms of lumbar spinal stenosis can have back pain and weakness and paresthesia (tingling, numbness) in the foot or leg. Pain can be triggered by standing for long periods of time or when walking. Pain usually eases when bending forward or sitting recline. This is similar to intermittent claudication where pain occurs in the legs after walking. Whereas intermittent vascular claudication is caused by too little blood flow to muscles in one or both legs, spinal stenosis is caused by a narrowing of the intervertebral spaces (neurogenic claudication). The pain in intermittent claudication tends to occur unilaterally in the calf whereas in lumbar spinal stenosis it tends to occur bilaterally in the buttocks or thighs. VERTEBRAL FRACTURES causing back pain may occur after a severe trauma, corticosteroid use or more often due to mild trauma in elderly patients with osteoporosis. The back pain is at the level of the fracture and radiates across the back and around the trunk and seldom into the legs. X-rays helps to confirm the diagnosis. ANKYLOSING SPONDYLITIS (AS) is an arthritic inflammatory disorder. The differentiating feature of AS is the involvement of the sacroiliac (SI) joints during the progression of the disease leading to sacroiliitis. This can eventually make the spine less flexible and result in a hunched-forward posture. Pain and stiffness is worse in the mornings and during the night and can improve with light exercise. The back pain from AS is inflammatory in nature and not mechanical. AS can also cause inflammation, pain, and stiffness in other areas of the body (shoulders, hips, ribs). Diagnosis is confirmed with positive family history, x-ray and the HLA-B27 (human leukocyte antigen B27) blood test. This is a blood test that checks for a specific protein in white blood cells that could trigger an auto-immune disease such as Ankylosing Spondylitis (AS). LUMBOSACRAL ARTHRITIS has a gradual onset and is usually associated with morning stiffness that improves within an hour. Lumbosacral motion can be limited. There is often arthritis in other joints. The back pain is worse with lateral bending and spine extension. X-rays can show a narrowing of the intervertebral disc space or can show bone spurs (osteophytes). Radiation of pain to the leg is uncommon. . Differential Diagnosis of Common Complaints, Sellers and Symons, 7th Edition, page 45, 46, 48",
      "exp_hant": "脊椎狹窄是脊椎椎間空間的狹窄，此狹窄會對脊神經造成壓迫。脊椎狹窄最常發生於下背與頸部，最常見的成因是與退化性椎間盤疾病（DDD）及骨關節炎相關的脊椎磨損退化性改變。X光與MRI有助於確診。腰椎脊椎狹窄的症狀可能包括背痛、無力，以及足部或腿部的感覺異常（刺痛、麻木）。疼痛可因久站或走路而誘發，通常在前彎或坐下後躺時緩解，這類似於走路後腿部疼痛的間歇性跛行。間歇性血管性跛行是因單側或雙側腿部肌肉血流不足所致，而脊椎狹窄則是因椎間空間狹窄所致（神經性跛行）。間歇性跛行的疼痛通常單側出現於小腿，而腰椎脊椎狹窄的疼痛則傾向雙側出現於臀部或大腿。椎體骨折造成的背痛，可能發生於嚴重外傷、使用皮質類固醇之後，或更常見於患有骨質疏鬆症的老年患者於輕微外傷後發生。背痛位於骨折的部位，並向背部及軀幹周圍放射，很少放射至腿部。X光有助於確診。僵直性脊椎炎（AS）是一種發炎性關節疾病，其鑑別特徵是疾病進展過程中薦髂關節（SI）的受累，導致薦髂關節炎，最終可能使脊椎失去柔軟度並造成駝背姿勢。疼痛與僵硬在早晨與夜間較為嚴重，並可因輕度運動而改善。AS的背痛屬發炎性而非機械性。AS也可能造成身體其他部位（肩、髖、肋骨）的發炎、疼痛與僵硬。診斷需結合陽性家族史、X光及HLA-B27（人類白血球抗原B27）血液檢測確認，這是一種檢查白血球中特定蛋白的血液檢測，該蛋白可能誘發如僵直性脊椎炎等自體免疫疾病。腰薦椎關節炎起病緩慢，通常伴隨於一小時內改善的晨僵，腰薦椎活動可能受限，且常合併其他關節炎。此類背痛在側彎與脊椎伸展時加劇。X光可顯示椎間盤空間狹窄或骨刺（骨贅），疼痛放射至腿部的情形並不常見。《常見主訴之鑑別診斷》，Sellers與Symons著，第7版，第45、46、48頁"
