      "exp_en": "A rotator cuff strain is a tear to any of the four rotator cuff muscles in the shoulder. It is a common injury in throwing and racket sports as well as painters and carpenters where there is repeated overhead motion. These groups of muscles and tendons surround the shoulder joint and keep the head of your humerus firmly within the shallow socket of the shoulder. A rotator cuff injury can manifest a dull ache in the shoulder, which is often worse when sleeping on the involved side. The patient describes the pain as a dull ache deep in the shoulder. There is difficulty in combing the hair or reaching behind the back. There is often arm weakness. Diagnosis is made via physical examination and can involve x-ray, MRI, and ultrasound. Bate's Guide to Physical Examination, 13th Edition, page 767; Bate's Guide to Physical Examination, 10th Edition, page 597",
