      "exp_en": "The abdominal bloating, diarrhea, and borderline iron deficiency anemia point to diagnosis of celiac disease. Celiac disease is an inflammatory disease of the small bowel due to an allergy or sensitivity to gluten containing foods such as wheat, rye, or barley. Other classic symptoms of celiac diseases include steatorrhea (excess fat in feces) and weight loss. Screening for celiac disease is done when the symptoms presented include abdominal pain/bloating, diarrhea, and weight loss. The anti-tTG, IgA is the most sensitive and specific blood test for celiac disease, with over 95% sensitivity, in detecting antibodies associated with celiac disease. Celiac disease is often overlooked as it has similar presentation as irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) and inflammatory bowel disease (IBD). The criteria for IBS includes recurrent abdominal pain or discomfort for at least three days per month during the past three months and associated with two or more of the following: 1) improvement with defecation. 2) onset associated with a change in frequency of stool. 3) onset associated with a change in the form of the stool. This patient falls within the criteria of IBS. Since 10% of those diagnosed with IBS also have celiac disease, these two disorders are not exclusive and need to be considered in the workup but the anti-tTG and IgA tests are conclusive for celiac disease. Patients with chronic loose stools can also have an inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) such as Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis. The hallmark of IBD include bloody diarrhea, urgency, steotorrhea, fever, abdominal pain, and weight loss. Both Cronh's and ulcerative colitis are diagnosed via endoscopy. If the initial diagnosis of celiac disease proves incorrect, then IBD needs to be considered. The normal hemoglobin range for men is 13.5 to 17.5 and for women it is 12.0 to 15.5. Normal HCT (hematocrit) for men is 38.8 to 50 and for women it is 34.9 to 44.5 percent. The normal ferritin range for men is 12 to 300 and for female it is 12 to 150. The lower the ferritin level, even if within the normal range, the more likely the patient has insufficient iron. Clinical Case Studies for the Family Nurse Practitioner, Leslie Neal-Boylan, 2011, page 313-315",
      "exp_hant": "腹脹、腹瀉及邊緣性缺鐵性貧血指向乳糜瀉的診斷。乳糜瀉是小腸因對含麩質食物（如小麥、黑麥或大麥）過敏或敏感所引起的發炎性疾病。乳糜瀉的其他典型症狀包括脂肪便（糞便中脂肪過多）及體重減輕。當出現腹痛／腹脹、腹瀉及體重減輕等症狀時，應進行乳糜瀉篩檢。anti-tTG、IgA是偵測乳糜瀉相關抗體最敏感且最具特異性的血液檢測，敏感度超過95%。乳糜瀉常因表現與大腸激躁症（IBS）及發炎性腸道疾病（IBD）相似而被忽略。IBS的診斷標準包括過去三個月內，每月至少三天出現反覆腹痛或不適，並合併以下兩項或以上：1）排便後症狀改善。2）發作與排便頻率改變有關。3）發作與糞便形狀改變有關。此患者符合IBS的診斷標準。由於10%被診斷為IBS的患者同時患有乳糜瀉，這兩種疾病並非互斥，需在評估中一併考量，但anti-tTG及IgA檢測對乳糜瀉具有決定性意義。慢性稀便患者也可能患有發炎性腸道疾病（IBD），如克隆氏症與潰瘍性結腸炎。IBD的特徵包括血便、急迫感、脂肪便、發燒、腹痛及體重減輕。克隆氏症與潰瘍性結腸炎皆須經內視鏡診斷。若最初的乳糜瀉診斷證實不成立，則需考慮IBD。男性血紅素正常範圍為13.5至17.5，女性為12.0至15.5。男性HCT（血比容）正常值為38.8至50，女性為34.9至44.5百分比。男性鐵蛋白正常範圍為12至300，女性為12至150。鐵蛋白數值越低，即使仍在正常範圍內，患者鐵質不足的可能性也越高。《Clinical Case Studies for the Family Nurse Practitioner》，Leslie Neal-Boylan著，2011年，第313-315頁"
