          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "The femur is the only bone of the human thigh. It is both the longest and the strongest bone in the human body. The human femur can resist forces of 1,800 to 2,500 pounds, so it is not easily fractured. The femur is responsible for bearing the largest percentage of body weight during normal weight-bearing activities. The stirrup (also called the stapes) is one of the three main bones (ossicles) in the middle ear connecting the eardrum to the cochlea. Ossicles refer to three bones in the middle ear: malleus or hammer, incus or anvil and stapes or stirrup. Starting from the outside of the ear to the innermost part of the ear, they are sequenced as malleus, incus and stapes. When a sound wave passes through the outer ear it vibrates the tympanic membrane of the eardrum. These vibrations are then transmitted by the malleus, through incus to the stapes. At the end, these vibrations reach to the oval window which is the opening of inner ear.",
      "exp_hant": "股骨是人類大腿唯一的骨骼，是人體中最長且最強壯的骨骼。人類股骨可承受1,800至2,500磅的力量，因此不易骨折。股骨負責在正常負重活動中承擔身體最大比例的重量。鐙骨（又稱為stapes）是中耳三塊主要聽小骨之一，連接鼓膜與耳蝸。聽小骨是指中耳的三塊骨頭：錘骨（malleus）、砧骨（incus）及鐙骨（stapes）。從耳朵外側到最內側，其順序依次為錘骨、砧骨及鐙骨。當聲波通過外耳時，會使鼓膜的鼓室膜產生振動，這些振動接著經由錘骨傳至砧骨，再傳至鐙骨。最後，這些振動抵達卵圓窗，即內耳的開口。"
