      "exp_en": "In a professional therapeutic or medical context (e.g., psychotherapy, psychiatry, or general medicine), a patient's outwardly seductive behavior often represents erotic transference, unconscious feelings redirected from past relationships onto the provider. This is common due to the intimate, trusting nature of the relationship and the power imbalance. Ignoring seductive behavior is considered inappropriate though not as severe as reciprocating because it can implicitly encourage escalation, it risks harm to the patient and it fails the health provider's ethical duty. Appropriate responses typically involve: 1) Calmly acknowledging the behavior; 2) Setting firm boundaries (e.g., \"I notice you're speaking in a more personal way; let's explore what that might mean for you\"); Seeking supervision/consultation if needed; 3) In extreme cases, consider referral or termination.",
