    {
      "number": 494,
      "question_en": "Which neurotransmitter is an amino acid?",
      "question_hant": "下列何者屬於胺基酸類的神經傳導物質？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "dopamine",
          "hant": "多巴胺",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "en": "serotonin",
          "hant": "血清素",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "glutamate",
          "hant": "麩胺酸（glutamate）",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "acetylcholine",
          "hant": "乙醯膽鹼",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "Neurotransmitters are chemicals which transmit signal-information across a synapse (a small gap between two neurons across which neurotransmitters travel) from one neuron to another targeted neuron. The most abundant neurotransmitter is glutamate. It is used by 80% of the neurons. Glutamate is associated with learning, memory, and brain development. It is an excitatory transmitter which means that it ensures that a neuron will fire by enhancing the electrical charge between neurons. A deficiency of glutamate has been linked to OCD (obsessive compulsive disorder), depression, autism, and schizophrenia. Acetylcholine was the first neurotransmitter discovered. In the peripheral nervous system, acetylcholine functions at the neuromuscular junction between the motor nerves and skeletal muscles thereby stimulating muscle contraction. In the central nervous system, acetylcholine is found primarily in between the neurons in many brain areas (cortex, basal ganglia, hypothalamus, etc) and is important for memory, cognition and motor control. Dopamine is involved with posture and voluntary movement, and emotional behavior. It is the neurotransmitter affected in Parkinson's disease. Insufficient dopamine can lead to stiffness, tremors or shaking, and other symptoms. Serotonin influences mood, sleep, and pain suppression. Drugs (SSRIs) used to treat depression act by blocking the recycling of serotonin by the sending neuron. Dopamine and serotonin are classified as monoamine based neurotransmitters. Acetylcholine is classified as choline based neurotransmitters.",
      "exp_hant": "神經傳導物質是一種化學物質，能將訊號資訊透過突觸（兩個神經元之間的微小間隙，神經傳導物質即經此傳遞）從一個神經元傳到目標神經元。含量最豐富的神經傳導物質是麩胺酸，約有80%的神經元使用它。麩胺酸與學習、記憶及腦部發育有關，屬於興奮性傳導物質，能增強神經元之間的電位差以確保神經元放電。麩胺酸不足與強迫症（OCD）、憂鬱症、自閉症及思覺失調症有關。乙醯膽鹼是最早被發現的神經傳導物質。在周邊神經系統中，乙醯膽鹼作用於運動神經與骨骼肌之間的神經肌肉接合處，藉此刺激肌肉收縮；在中樞神經系統中，乙醯膽鹼主要存在於許多腦區（如皮質、基底核、下視丘等）的神經元之間，對記憶、認知及運動控制相當重要。多巴胺與姿勢、隨意運動及情緒行為有關，是帕金森氏症中受影響的神經傳導物質；多巴胺不足可能導致僵硬、震顫或顫抖等症狀。血清素則影響情緒、睡眠與疼痛抑制，用於治療憂鬱症的藥物（SSRI）即透過阻斷傳遞端神經元回收血清素而發揮作用。多巴胺與血清素屬於單胺類神經傳導物質，乙醯膽鹼則屬於膽鹼類神經傳導物質。"
    },
