      "exp_en": "This is the most complete and accurate answer. Venous insufficiency has thickening and hardening of the skin, varicose veins, leg edema. Arterial insufficiency rarely causes edema. Leg edema occurs in venous insufficiency. Arterial insufficiency occurs in a condition that can slow down or stop blood flow through your arteries. Arteries transport blood from the heart to the rest of the body. The most common cause of arterial insufficiency is atherosclerosis. Blood flow may also stop due to a blood clot which can then travel (embolus). The symptoms depend on which arteries are affected: affected heart arteries can have chest pain or a MI. Affected brain arteries can lead to a stroke. Leg arteries affected can have leg cramping (claudication) or cold-pale legs. Digestive arteries affected can have epigastric pain after a meal. A vascular ultrasound is used to evaluate venous or arterial insufficiency of the extremities, the strength of the venous system of the extremities, and occlusion of the veins or arteries of an extremities.",
