        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "GABA receptors",
          "hant": "GABA受體",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "acetyl choline receptors",
          "hant": "乙醯膽鹼受體",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "Benzodiazepines (benzos for short) and barbiturates act as an agonist (activate) for GABA (gamma Amino Butyric Acid), a neurotransmitter that inhibits excitatory neurotransmitters (norepinephrine, serotonin, acetyl choline, dopamine) involved with alertness. BENZODIAZEPINES include diazepam (Valium), clonazapan (Klonopin), lorazepam (Ativan), temazepam (Restoril), flunitrazepam (Rohypnol), triazolam (Halcion), and alprazolam (Xanax). These drugs produce calm, relaxed muscles, and sleepiness. They have a sedative, hypnotic (sleep-inducing), anxiolytic (anti-anxiety), anticonvulsant, and muscle relaxing properties. Overdose symptoms include severe drowsiness, confusion, and fainting. Overdosing with alcohol and other drugs can lead to coma, respiratory suppression, and even death. Benzos are often used for short-term treatment of panic, anxiety, insomnia or muscle spasms. Benzos are used short-term and have a high risk for dependency when used in long-term situation (over 3 months). Gradual discontinuation of benzos is done under medical supervision to counteract withdrawal symptoms (seizures, tremors, muscle cramping, vomiting, sweating, depression, anxiety, insomnia). BARBITURATES include phenobarbital, pentobarbital, and thiopental (sodium pentothal, sodium amytal).Barbiturates (downers, barbs) are sedatives that clinically treat seizures, insomnia and anxiety. Their clinical use has been increasingly replaced by use of benzodiazepines due to highly addictive properties. They are now more commonly prescribed for anesthesia and sedation. Barbiturates produce euphoria, relaxed contentment sedation, impaired coordination, lowered inhibitions, and dizziness Overdose produces respiratory depression, speech slurring, and staggering/inability to walk. Barbiturates are used for very short-term and are very addictive and can be life threatening. Withdrawal symptoms can include anxiety, muscle pain, elevated heart and respiratory rate, confusion, hallucinations, or seizures. Medical detox treatment is recommended for barbiturate addiction. The biggest difference between barbiturate and benzos is that the barbiturates have a much more rapid habituation and the withdrawal can be very serious. Barbiturates can also be lethal at low doses. Benzos have all the same risks except that they are less likely to be lethal. Tolerance for benzos does not develop that quickly. tcmtests.com, July 2020",
      "exp_hant": "苯二氮平類（簡稱benzos）與巴比妥類藥物皆作用為GABA（γ-胺基丁酸）的促效劑（activate），GABA是一種能抑制與警覺性有關的興奮性神經傳導物質（正腎上腺素、血清素、乙醯膽鹼、多巴胺）的神經傳導物質。苯二氮平類藥物包括diazepam（Valium，煩寧）、clonazepam（Klonopin）、lorazepam（Ativan）、temazepam（Restoril）、flunitrazepam（Rohypnol）、triazolam（Halcion）及alprazolam（Xanax，贊安諾）。這類藥物能產生鎮靜、肌肉放鬆及嗜睡效果，具有鎮靜、安眠（誘導睡眠）、抗焦慮、抗痙攣及肌肉鬆弛的作用。過量服用的症狀包括嚴重嗜睡、意識混亂及暈厥；若與酒精或其他藥物併用過量，則可能導致昏迷、呼吸抑制甚至死亡。苯二氮平類藥物常用於短期治療恐慌症、焦慮症、失眠或肌肉痙攣，若長期使用（超過3個月）則有很高的成癮風險。逐步減量停藥須在醫療監督下進行，以避免出現戒斷症狀（癲癇發作、震顫、肌肉抽筋、嘔吐、流汗、憂鬱、焦慮、失眠）。巴比妥類藥物包括phenobarbital（苯巴比妥）、pentobarbital（戊巴比妥）及thiopental（硫噴妥鈉，即sodium pentothal、sodium amytal）。巴比妥類藥物（俗稱downers、barbs）屬於鎮靜劑，臨床上用於治療癲癇發作、失眠及焦慮，但由於其高度成癮性，臨床用途已逐漸被苯二氮平類藥物取代，目前較常用於麻醉與鎮靜。巴比妥類藥物會產生欣快感、放鬆滿足的鎮靜感、協調能力受損、抑制能力降低及頭暈；過量則會導致呼吸抑制、言語含糊及步態不穩／無法行走。巴比妥類藥物僅適合非常短期使用，成癮性極高，且可能危及生命。戒斷症狀可能包括焦慮、肌肉疼痛、心跳與呼吸速率升高、意識混亂、幻覺或癲癇發作，巴比妥類藥物成癮建議接受醫療戒毒治療。巴比妥類藥物與苯二氮平類藥物最大的差異在於，巴比妥類藥物產生耐受性的速度快得多，且戒斷反應可能非常嚴重，即使低劑量也可能致命；苯二氮平類藥物雖具有相同風險，但致命可能性較低，且耐受性發展速度較慢。tcmtests.com，2020年7月"
    },
