      ],
      "exp_en": "Like morphine, heroin, and other opioids, fentanyl binds to the opiate receptors. Fentanyl is the most potent opioid for use in medical treatment. According to the DEA, it is 50 to 100 times more potent than morphine and 30 to 50 times more potent than heroin. The music star Prince died from a fentanyl overdose in April 2016. Pharmaceutical fentanyl is a synthetic opioid pain reliever which has been approved for treating severe pain, typically advanced cancer pain. It is 50x more potent than heroin and 100x more potent than morphine. Other prescription drugs associated with the opioid epidemic include oxycodone, hydrocodone, and methadone. From 1999 to 2015, more than 183,000 individuals have died in the U.S. from overdoses related to prescription opioids. Over 1,000 people every day are treated in emergency departments for prescription opioids abuse. Opioid drugs can be categorized as natural opiates (opium morphine, codeine), semisynthetic opiates (heroin, oxycodone, hydrocodone), or synthetic opiates (methadone, fentanyl). Opioid drugs bind opioid receptors in the brain, spinal cord, and other body areas. Opioid drug reduce sending pain signals to the brain and reduce pain sensations. Opioid drug side effects include respiratory depression, nausea, sedation, euphoria or dysphoria, constipation and itching. A patient with an opioid overdose has pinpoint pupils, loss of consciousness, and respiratory depression. The symptoms of opioid withdrawal include strong cravings for the drug, cramps, sweating, chills, and vomiting. Opioid drugs can be classified as Schedule I, Schedule II, and Schedule III. Schedule I drugs (heroin, LSD, marijuana, ecstasy, peyote) have no currently accepted medical use and a high potential for abuse. Schedule II drugs (cocaine, methamphetamine, methadone, hydromorphone, Demerol, oxycodone, fentanyl, Adderall) have a high potential for abuse that can lead to severe psychological or physical dependence. Schedule III drugs (less than 90mg of codeine per dosage unit, ketamine, anabolic steroids, testosterone) have a moderate to low potential for physical and psychological dependence. www.cdc.gov",
      "exp_hant": "如同嗎啡、海洛因及其他鴉片類藥物，芬太尼會與鴉片受體結合。芬太尼是目前醫療用途中效力最強的鴉片類藥物。根據美國緝毒局（DEA）的資料，其效力是嗎啡的50至100倍，是海洛因的30至50倍。已故音樂巨星「王子」（Prince）於2016年4月因芬太尼過量而死亡。藥用芬太尼是一種合成鴉片類止痛藥，經核准用於治療劇烈疼痛，通常用於晚期癌症疼痛。其效力是海洛因的50倍、嗎啡的100倍。與類鴉片濫用疫情相關的其他處方藥物包括羥可酮（oxycodone）、氫可酮（hydrocodone）及美沙酮。1999年至2015年間，美國已有超過183,000人因處方鴉片類藥物相關的過量用藥而死亡。每天有超過1,000人因處方鴉片類藥物濫用而在急診室接受治療。鴉片類藥物可分為天然鴉片類（鴉片、嗎啡、可待因）、半合成鴉片類（海洛因、羥可酮、氫可酮）及合成鴉片類（美沙酮、芬太尼）。鴉片類藥物會與腦部、脊髓及身體其他部位的鴉片受體結合，減少痛覺訊號傳至大腦，降低疼痛感。鴉片類藥物的副作用包括呼吸抑制、噁心、鎮靜、欣快感或情緒不悅、便秘及搔癢。鴉片類藥物過量的病人會出現針尖狀瞳孔、意識喪失及呼吸抑制。鴉片類藥物戒斷的症狀包括強烈的藥物渴求、抽搐、盜汗、發冷及嘔吐。鴉片類藥物可分為第一級（Schedule I）、第二級（Schedule II）及第三級（Schedule III）管制藥物。第一級藥物（海洛因、LSD、大麻、搖頭丸、烏羽玉）目前被認為無公認的醫療用途且濫用風險高。第二級藥物（古柯鹼、甲基安非他命、美沙酮、氫嗎啡酮、配西汀、羥可酮、芬太尼、阿德拉）濫用風險高，可能導致嚴重的心理或生理依賴。第三級藥物（每劑量單位含可待因少於90毫克者、氯胺酮、合成代謝類固醇、睾固酮）造成生理及心理依賴的風險屬中至低度。www.cdc.gov"
    },
