      "exp_en": "A bullae is a cavity filled with air or fluid. Fluid bullaes can be a common occurrence while a hemorrhagic (blood) bullae is less common. Hemorrhagic bullaes can occur with patients on anti-coagulant medication or supplements such as fish oil or vitamin E that can act as blood thinners. Tumefaction (swelling) and ecchymosis are common reactions to cupping. Hemorrhagic bullaes can form scabs before they heal which can take up to two weeks. Having the skin opened through cupping increases the risk of infection. Patients should be given instructions on using antibiotic cream and maintaining the skin clean and protected with a sterile gauze. If a patient develops red subcutaneous nodules (panniculitis) within the round area where cupping was applied, do not do any more cupping and refer to a physician. Obtain patient consent before applying cupping. It is recommended, that a handout explaining cupping that includes changes to the skin and time line for resolution be given to the patient. Clean Needle Technique, 7th Edition, page 33, 115, 117",
      "exp_hant": "水泡（bullae）是一種充滿空氣或液體的腔室。液性水泡是常見現象，而出血性（血性）水泡則較不常見。出血性水泡可能發生於服用抗凝血藥物或魚油、維生素E等具血液稀釋作用之補充品的病人身上。腫脹（tumefaction）與瘀斑是拔罐常見的反應。出血性水泡在癒合前可能形成痂皮，過程可能長達兩週。透過拔罐使皮膚破損，會增加感染的風險，因此應指導病人使用抗生素軟膏，並保持皮膚清潔並以無菌紗布保護。若病人在拔罐部位的圓形範圍內出現紅色皮下結節（脂膜炎），應停止再進行拔罐，並轉診至醫師。施行拔罐前應取得病人同意，建議提供說明拔罐後皮膚變化及恢復時程的說明單張給病人。《潔淨針法》，第7版，第33、115、117頁"
