      "exp_en": "The CDC does not accept household bleach (sodium hypochlorite) to be used as a disinfecting agent in a health care setting. However, commercial EPA approved dilutions of sodium hypochlorite can be used, usually within 24 hours of mixing. Hypochlorite dilutions should be made fresh daily. Glutaraldehyde is a high-level disinfectant for instruments (endoscopes, respiratory equipment, dialyzers, etc) that enter the body. Glutaraldehyde should not be used for cleaning surfaces. It is expensive and gives off vapors (need hood ventilation). Stabilized 6-25% hydrogen peroxide is capable of high-level disinfection. Store bought hydrogen peroxide is at the 3% level and will not effectively sterilize. 6-25% hydrogen peroxide is not toxic and does not require hood ventilation. The brand name Sporox is an FDA approved hydrogen peroxide solution. Phenol is a low-level disinfectant suitable for surfaces. Clean Needle Technique, 7th Edition, page 191",
