      ],
      "exp_en": "The immune system comprises of a non-specific immune response (innate immune system) and a specific immune response (adaptive immune system). When the immune system encounters a pathogen the normal sequence to first activates the innate immune system (first line of defense) followed by the activation of the adaptive immune system (second line of defense). During the first 96 hours the innate immune system is activated through natural killer cells, macrophages, neutrophils, dendritic cells, mast cells, basophils, and eosinophils. This is a global and non-specific immune response to the pathogen. The innate system has the same reaction independent of what type of pathogen. After about 96 hours the adaptive immune system is also activated through T-lymphocytes (T-cells) and B-lymphocytes (B-cells). This is a specific immune response through the identification of a specific antigen and producing antibodies specific to that antigen. In this sequence the immunity is achieve by actually catching that disease (innate system) and then producing specific antibodies (adaptive system). Vaccines are given by injection. They directly introduce dead or weakened viruses or bacteria or pieces of them (called antigen) into the body. An antigen is a molecule that is capable of stimulating an immune response in our body. Vaccines bypass the normal sequence of first activating the innate immune system (first line of defense) followed by the activation of the adaptive immune system (second line of defense). The purpose of vaccines is to directly activate the adaptive immune system by introducing pathogens into the body. The adaptive system then produces antibodies to the antigen. The T-cells of the adaptive system identify the antigen and signal to the B-cells to produce specific antibodies to that antigen. These antibodies work only on the antigen against which they were generated. T-cells are also called memory cells. That is, if the immune system encounters the same antigen in the future, it recognizes them and signals the B-cells to produce antibodies to attack them again. Vaccines provide immunity by bypassing the innate system and activating the adaptive system directly. tcmtests.com, February 2020",
      "exp_hant": "免疫系統包含非特異性免疫反應（先天性免疫系統）與特異性免疫反應（適應性免疫系統）。當免疫系統遇到病原體時，正常順序是先啟動先天性免疫系統（第一道防線），接著才啟動適應性免疫系統（第二道防線）。在最初的96小時內，先天性免疫系統會透過自然殺手細胞、巨噬細胞、嗜中性球、樹突細胞、肥大細胞、嗜鹼性球及嗜酸性球被啟動，這是一種對病原體整體、非特異性的免疫反應，先天性系統對任何類型的病原體都有相同的反應。約96小時後，適應性免疫系統也會透過T淋巴球（T細胞）與B淋巴球（B細胞）被啟動，這是一種特異性免疫反應，透過辨識特定抗原並產生針對該抗原的特異性抗體。在此順序下，免疫力是先透過實際感染該疾病（先天性系統）獲得，接著才產生特異性抗體（適應性系統）。疫苗經由注射給予，直接將已死亡或減毒的病毒或細菌，或其部分成分（稱為抗原）引入體內。抗原是一種能夠刺激體內免疫反應的分子。疫苗會繞過先啟動先天性免疫系統（第一道防線）、再啟動適應性免疫系統（第二道防線）的正常順序。疫苗的目的，是透過將病原體引入體內，直接啟動適應性免疫系統。適應性系統隨後會針對該抗原產生抗體。適應性系統中的T細胞會辨識該抗原，並向B細胞發出訊號，使其產生針對該抗原的特異性抗體，這些抗體僅對產生它們的那個抗原有效。T細胞也被稱為記憶細胞，也就是說，若免疫系統未來再次遇到相同抗原，便會辨識出它，並向B細胞發出訊號，促使其再次產生抗體加以攻擊。疫苗透過繞過先天性系統、直接啟動適應性系統來提供免疫力。tcmtests.com，2020年2月"
    },
