      "exp_en": "A wrist drop is pathognomonic of radial neuropathy that affects the posterior cord of the brachial plexus from fibers of C5. Wrist drop is the most common presentation and the most typical radial neuropathy at the spiral groove where there is weakness of the wrist and finger and extension of the radial brachialis with preservation of the triceps and deltoid function. Upon examination you would find the deep tendon reflex of the brachial radialis is weak whereas the triceps would be normal. This brachial radialis is an elbow flexor that is best examined in the arm when it is semi-pronated. Its weakness is often supported by the absence of the normal bulge with attempt to flex the elbow. There is a loss of sensation and variable pain in the radial distribution of the hand with no weakness in the hand per se. Saunders Manual of Neurological Practice, Evans Randolph, 2003, page 625",
