      ],
      "exp_en": "Dysmenorrhea refers to the condition with menstrual cramps and painful periods. There are two types of dysmenorrhea: primary dysmenorrhea and secondary dysmenorrhea. Primary dysmenorrhea refers to menstrual cramping and pain in the lower abdomen that usually occurs before or during menstrual periods. The primary dysmenorrhea is so common that the occurrence of it is about ninety percent. Secondary dysmenorrhea refers to the menstrual pain in the presence of pelvic diseases. Most often, the secondary dysmenorrhea condition occurs due to endometriosis, which results in infection, pelvic pain and internal bleeding. Other causes for secondary dysmenorrhea are pelvic inflammatory disease, uterine fibroids, tumors, or polyps. The pain usually begins a few days before the onset of the menstrual periods and remains for several days after the periods. Endometriosis is a painful disorder arising from the tissue (endometrium) that normally lines the inside of the uterus. The endometrium grows outside of the uterus. Endometriosis can involve the ovaries, fallopian tubes, and the pelvic tissue. Although cramping is common during the menstrual period, women with endometriosis describe menstrual pain that is worse than usual and that the pain increases over time. Symptoms of endometriosis may include lower back and abdominal pain, painful sex, pain with bowel movements or urination, excess uterine bleeding, and bleeding between periods. Diagnosis of endometriosis is confirmed via pelvic exam, ultrasound, MRI, and laparoscopy (thin tube with tiny light and video to look into the pelvis). Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) is an infection of the female reproductive organs. Normally occurs when sexually transmitted bacteria spread into the uterus, fallopian tubes or ovaries. Symptoms of PID can include heavy vaginal discharges, abnormal uterine bleeding, painful sex, fever and chills, or painful/difficult urination. Diagnosis of PID is confirmed with blood and urine tests which will show an increase of WBC, ultrasound to image the reproductive organs, and laparoscopy. Clinical Case Studies for the Family Nurse Practitioner, Leslie Neal-Boylan, 2011, page 147-149",
      "exp_hant": "經痛（dysmenorrhea）是指經期出現痙攣與疼痛的狀況，可分為原發性經痛與續發性經痛兩種。原發性經痛是指通常發生於月經來潮前或月經期間的下腹絞痛，非常常見，約佔所有經痛的九成。續發性經痛則是指伴隨骨盆腔疾病而出現的經痛，最常見的原因是子宮內膜異位症，可導致感染、骨盆腔疼痛與體內出血；其他造成續發性經痛的原因還包括骨盆腔發炎疾病、子宮肌瘤、腫瘤或息肉。這類疼痛通常在月經來潮前幾天開始，並持續至經期結束後數天。子宮內膜異位症是一種因原本應襯於子宮內壁的組織（子宮內膜）生長至子宮以外部位而引起的疼痛性疾病，可能侵犯卵巢、輸卵管與骨盆腔組織。雖然經期絞痛很常見，但子宮內膜異位症患者所描述的經痛通常比一般情況更為嚴重，且會隨時間逐漸加劇。子宮內膜異位症的症狀可能包括下背與腹部疼痛、性交疼痛、排便或排尿疼痛、經血量過多，以及經期外出血。子宮內膜異位症的診斷可透過骨盆腔檢查、超音波、核磁共振與腹腔鏡（一種帶有小型光源與攝影功能的細管，用以觀察骨盆腔內部）確診。骨盆腔發炎疾病（PID）是女性生殖器官的感染，通常是因性傳染細菌擴散至子宮、輸卵管或卵巢所引起。PID的症狀可能包括大量陰道分泌物、異常子宮出血、性交疼痛、發燒與畏寒，或排尿疼痛／困難。PID的診斷可透過血液與尿液檢查（顯示白血球增加）、生殖器官超音波影像，以及腹腔鏡檢查確診。《家庭護理師臨床病例研究》，Leslie Neal-Boylan著，2011年，頁147-149"
    },
    {
      "number": 805,
      "question_en": "Gary is a 50 year-old stockbroker. One cold and rainy day he arrives at the doctor's office complaining of right foot pain. He is wearing soft mocassin slippers and cries out in pain when the doctor attempts to remove the right slipper. The right big toe is red, swollen, and inflamed extending from the toe into the ball of the foot. The rest of the foot is not involved. The left foot is unremarkable. All other joints are normal. Physical exam reveals a slight tenderness to palpation on the right kidney. Last month he went to the ER to pass a kidney stone. He was told to drink lots of water and has had no problems since. He usually drinks one or two cocktails after work and last night he drank more than usual. What tests should be performed?",
      "question_hant": "蓋瑞（Gary）是一位50歲的股票經紀人。在一個寒冷多雨的日子，他前來診所求診，主訴右腳疼痛。他穿著柔軟的莫卡辛便鞋，當醫師試圖脫下他右腳的鞋子時，他痛得大叫。右腳大拇趾發紅、腫脹且發炎，範圍從腳趾延伸至腳掌前部，腳部其餘部位未受影響，左腳則無異狀。其他關節皆正常。理學檢查發現右側腎臟觸診時有輕微壓痛。上個月他曾因排出腎結石而至急診就醫，當時被告知要多喝水，此後便未再出現問題。他平時下班後通常會喝一到兩杯雞尾酒，昨晚則喝得比平常多。應進行哪些檢查？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "X-ray of foot, serum uric acid, CBC",
          "hant": "足部X光、血清尿酸、全血球計數（CBC）",
